# Foundational Product Model

Coreside consists of two major layers.

## Protected Core

The Protected Core is the immutable and trusted foundation required for the product to remain functional, recoverable, secure, and identifiable.

It includes:

- Product name
- Coreside logos
- Application icons
- Dock icons
- Brand mappings
- Base Settings structure
- Appearance controls
- Accessibility controls
- Data controls
- Provider credential management
- OS credential storage
- Search-budget enforcement
- Search-provider infrastructure
- Crawl infrastructure
- Database engine
- Database migrations
- Protected navigation
- Recovery controls
- Security boundaries
- Validation infrastructure
- Versioning infrastructure
- Export security
- Automation safety limits
- Readability enforcement
- Capability-pack trust registry
- Protected agent instructions
- Application update infrastructure

The runtime agent must not be able to:

- Delete the Protected Core
- Rename protected resources
- Replace protected resources
- Hide security settings
- Disable credential protections
- Remove recovery controls
- Modify logos or Dock icons
- Disable validation
- Increase protected resource limits
- Bypass provider budgets
- Disable readability enforcement
- Grant generated surfaces arbitrary system access

## User-Editable Layer

The User-Editable Layer is where Coreside becomes personalized.

It may include:

- Personal tools
- Inline surfaces
- Personal Home
- User dashboards
- Workspace panels
- Project panels
- Added Settings
- Template Settings
- Wallpapers
- Custom layouts
- User sidebar shortcuts
- Automations
- Imported media
- User-created capability configurations
- Project-specific interfaces
- Games
- Trackers
- Forms
- Charts
- Clocks
- Calculators
- Study tools
- Research tools

The AI may create and modify this layer through trusted, validated operations.

The boundary between the Protected Core and User-Editable Layer must remain explicit in:

- Types
- Database records
- Rust validation
- Frontend validation
- Agent prompts
- Tests
- Documentation

Do not rely on visual separation alone.

